# ---- Exported app data: the one rule here that is not housekeeping ----
#
# A backup written by the app's own export contains the logbook verbatim, and
# shift notes carry real names, dates of service and physicians. This
# repository is public, and a blob committed by mistake stays reachable in
# history after the file is deleted -- removing it later does not undo the
# disclosure. Matched on the export's own naming so that a stray `git add -A`
# cannot pick one up.
nodrift-backup-*.json
*-backup-*.json
*.backup.json

# ---- Secrets ----
#
# Nothing here needs a secret today: the app is static, so the browser
# downloads everything it runs, and the Supabase publishable key is public by
# design -- guarded by row level security rather than by being hidden. These
# patterns exist so that if a script or build step ever does introduce a real
# secret, leaving it out is already the default.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
secrets.json
service-role*

# ---- Tooling and hosts ----
node_modules/
.vercel/
.netlify/
*.log
npm-debug.log*

# ---- Editors and OS ----
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# ---- The CDP test harness ----
#
# nodrift-harness/ lives inside the repo so the tests travel with the app they
# test, but it is deliberately never committed. It is a scratch rig: it holds
# a live test account's fixtures, writes screenshots and mutation scratch
# files beside itself, and its scripts are LF while everything tracked here is
# CRLF. Deployment is the other half of the reason -- the repo root is the web
# root on Vercel, so anything tracked here is published, and a harness that
# names the Supabase project and the test account has no business being served
# to the public.
nodrift-harness/
